<p>Henry Wheatley&#8217;s 1878 monograph <a href="https://archive.org/details/whatisanindexaf00englgoog/page/n59/mode/2up">&#8220;What Is an Index?&#8221;</a> gives a list of &#8220;titles of books that have given trouble to bibiographers&#8221; or been assigned to the wrong subjects:</p>
<ul>
<li>Maria Edgeworth&#8217;s &#8220;Essay on Irish Bulls&#8221; and a treatise on the Great Seal of the United States have both been listed under Zoology.</li>
<li>A description of Napier&#8217;s bones, John Napier&#8217;s manual calculating device, has been listed under Anatomy.</li>
<li>&#8220;Under the Microscope,&#8221; poet Algernon Charles Swinburne&#8217;s 1872 invective against critic Robert Buchanan, has been listed under Optical Instruments.</li>
<li>A volume of poetry titled <em>The Viol and the Lute</em> has been listed under Musical Instruments.</li>
<li>John Ruskin&#8217;s <em>Notes on the Construction of Sheepfolds</em>, an 1851 pamphlet on divinity, has been listed under Agriculture.</li>
<li>Scottish minister William McEwen&#8217;s writings on biblical types have been listed under Printing.</li>
</ul>
<p>Best: German naturalist Johann Heinrich Linck&#8217;s <em>De stellis marinis liber singularis</em>, a treatise on sea stars, has been listed under Astronomy.</p>
